# Data files are byte-exact artefacts with published SHA-256 digests. Any line-ending
# translation would change those bytes on checkout and break `verify_release.py
# --strict-checksums` on a different platform than the one that built them.
* -text

# Source and docs are normalised to LF so the repo is consistent across platforms.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.cff   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf diff
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf

# But not these: they carry published digests or are data payloads.
data/**/*.json  -text
data/**/*.jsonl -text
checksums/SHA256SUMS -text

*.parquet binary
*.csv     binary
*.txt     binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Git LFS
#
# WHY. `data/corpus/recipes_structured.parquet` reached 99.1 MB against GitHub's hard
# 100 MB per-file limit, and it grows with every added column -- three were added on
# 2026-09-05 alone. Without LFS the next rebuild makes the repository unpushable.
#
# HISTORY IS SAFE. The largest blob across all existing commits is 49.0 MB, so no
# `lfs migrate` and no history rewrite is required. LFS applies from the next commit.
#
# BYTE-EXACTNESS, which is what the `* -text` rule above protects. LFS stores and restores
# the object verbatim and performs no line-ending translation; each line below carries its
# own `-text`. Verified: staging the 99 MB parquet through the filter left its working-tree
# SHA-256 unchanged, and the LFS OID IS that SHA-256, so it is directly comparable to
# checksums/SHA256SUMS.
#
# !! REQUIRES A REPOSITORY SETTING !!
# GitHub omits LFS content from source archives BY DEFAULT, and Zenodo archives the zipball.
# Settings -> General -> Archives -> [x] Include Git LFS objects in archives
# must be ON, or the Zenodo record fills with 130-byte pointer stubs, silently. The release
# workflow downloads its own zipball and fails if that happens, so the setting cannot be off
# without the build saying so.
*.parquet filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
#
# The four bulk files below are listed per DIRECTORY-GLOB, not per exact path. The earlier
# exact-path form covered `synthetic_interactions/` only, so when `synthetic_interactions_v3/`
# was added its 24.6 MB `interactions.csv` -- LARGER than the v1 file the rule was written for
# -- matched nothing and would have been committed raw. A generation-numbered directory is
# going to keep happening; the glob covers the next one too.
data/synthetic_interactions*/interactions.csv filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
data/synthetic_interactions*/users.csv        filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
data/synthetic_interactions*/train.txt        filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
data/synthetic_interactions*/kg_final.txt     filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
data/synthetic_interactions*/test.txt         filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
